 Let’s start by expanding $a^3+b^3$ using the sum of cubes formula. We have
\begin{align*}
a^3+b^3 &= (a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2) \\
&= (a+b)(a^2+b^2-ab) \\
&= (a+b)((a+b)^2-3ab)
\end{align*}
We know that $a-b=6$, so we can write $a=b+6$. Substituting this into the equation, we get
\begin{align*}
a^3+b^3 &= (b+6+6)((b+6+6)^2-3b(b+6)) \\
&= (2b+12)(b^2+12b+36-3b^2-18b) \\
&= (2b+12)(-2b^2+18b+36)
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\frac{a^3+b^3}{a+b} &= \frac{(2b+12)(-2b^2+18b+36)}{2b+12} \\
&= -2b^2+18b+36
\end{align*}
Next, we need to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of $-2b^2+18b+36$ and $ab$. We know that $a-b=6$, so we can write $a=b+6$. Substituting this into the equation, we get
\begin{align*}
-2b^2+18b+36 &= -2b^2+18b+36 \\
&= -2b^2+18b+36 \\
&= 2(b+9)(b-6)
\end{align*}
We want to find $\text{gcd}(-2b^2+18b+36, ab) = 9$. Since $a-b=6$, we know that $a=6+b$. Substituting this into the equation, we get
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(-2b^2+18b+36, ab) &= \text{gcd}(2(b+9)(b-6), (b+6)b) \\
&= \text{gcd}(2(b+9)(b-6), (b+6)(b+6)) \\
&= \text{gcd}(2(b+9)(b-6), (b+6)^2) \\
&= 9
\end{align*}
This means that $b+6=9$, so $b=3$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of $b$ is $\boxed{3}$. </answer>

